# World Model Conventions

Source of truth: `PROJECT_BRIEF.md`. Prefer that file over guessing when ambiguous.

## Hardware / Runtime

- Target device is **CUDA** (NVIDIA RTX 5080, Blackwell). Not MPS.
- Verify `torch.cuda.is_available()` before training work.
- Install PyTorch from the **CUDA 12.8+** wheel index, never the default PyPI
  CPU wheel. RTX 50-series needs sm_120 kernels.
- Default mixed precision is **bf16 AMP**. Enable TF32 / cuDNN benchmark via
  `training.device.configure_runtime`.
- Fit the XL CNN + 3 decode heads in **16 GiB VRAM** (batch 16 × seq 32 + bf16).
  Desktop compositor already uses ~1.5 GiB. seq 64 filled the card; if a step
  OOMs, drop `batch_size` 16 → 8. Replay lives in the 32 GiB system RAM.

## Tensor Convention

- All tensors are `[batch, time, features]` unless a docstring or variable name says otherwise.
- Document exact input/output shapes in every public function docstring.

## Prior vs Posterior Naming (never conflate)

- `z_posterior` / `z_post_t`: from deterministic state `h` **and** the encoder embedding (world-model training on real data).
- `z_prior` / `z_prior_t`: from `h` **alone** (imagination rollouts).
- Never name a latent just `z_t` — always disambiguate prior vs posterior.

## Scope

- Environment: Crafter only (`CrafterReward-v1`). MiniGrid is optional for early debugging.
- No external model/API calls in the training or inference path.
- Experiment tracking is local TensorBoard only.

## Git

- Work and push directly on `main` — do not create feature branches.
- Prefer small, frequent commits with why-focused messages.
- Keep `main` working; do not push known-broken code.

## Training runs

- **Never launch training (`scripts/train_world_model.py` or equivalent) from
  the agent chat/shell**, including "quick" background runs, even if asked to
  "fix" or "do whatever it takes." The user runs all real training themselves
  via the Jupyter notebooks (`notebooks/05_train_world_model.ipynb`, etc.) so
  they can watch it live and stop it whenever they want.
- Short smoke tests (a few dozen steps, in a scratch config, to check shapes
  or throughput after a code/config change) are fine and expected — just
  don't let them run long or stand in for a real training run.
  `scripts/smoke_cuda_step.py` is the CUDA VRAM/throughput check.
- If a fix needs real training to validate, make the code/config change,
  verify it with a smoke test, then hand off to the user to run the actual
  training pass themselves and report back results.
